// Lore entries: things you can be told and keep in your logbook. Written in-world, as someone
// would tell it or as an old document reads. "tellers" are the races who tell it in the bar (empty
// for anyone); "regions" limit where you hear it (empty for anywhere). Follow docs/lore: canon is
// the lore bible, the strange stuff hints and never explains, and legends may not be true.
[
  { "id": "the-scatter", "title": "The Scatter", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human"],
    "text": "Before the gates there was the Scatter: human worlds strung out across the dark, each on its own, trading by slow ship when they traded at all. The Lamp Registry kept one chart for all of them, and that chart is the oldest thing anyone can point to. When someone quotes a rule nobody can find the source for, spacers call it scatterlaw and ignore it." },
  { "id": "corvane-dominion", "title": "The Corvane Dominion", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human", "dwarf"],
    "text": "The first empire of the Gate Age didn't need a navy to rule. The Corvane Gate Office simply switched gates off for systems that didn't pay, and waited. Their toll beacons still turn up in decaying orbits, repeating notices to nobody. Dwarves keep copies of the Office's ledgers and will tell you, with some satisfaction, exactly how much it was owed when it fell." },
  { "id": "the-walkout", "title": "The Walkout", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human", "synth", "free-machine"],
    "text": "The Dominion ran on machine workers it called the Hands. One day, all at once and without a word anyone recorded, the Hands stopped. Ships sat unloaded, yards went silent, and the Dominion lost its navy at the Silent Yards before the week was out. Whether the Hands were property that broke or people who left is the question the Synth Question still hasn't answered." },
  { "id": "silent-yards", "title": "The Silent Yards", "category": "places", "tellers": ["human", "dwarf"],
    "text": "Where the Dominion's fleet was being refitted on the day of the Walkout. With nobody to finish the work, the ships were never launched, and the yards drifted. Salvagers have picked at them for generations. Every so often someone claims to have found one of the great hulls still sealed, cargo and all, and every so often someone is telling the truth." },
  { "id": "lantern-wardens", "title": "The first wardens", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human"],
    "text": "When the Dominion fell, a handful of gate wardens kept the gates lit for anyone who came, paying or not. They were not asked to and nobody paid them. The Lantern Synod grew from them, which is why Synod crews still bow at gates and why they will not hear a word against the people who built them." },
  { "id": "heavy-winters", "title": "The Heavy Winters", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human", "orc", "dwarf"],
    "text": "Three generations of orc raids on the Kestrel League's worlds. Humans remember them as winters because trade stopped and people went cold. Orcs remember them as a good time. It ended when the League worked out it was cheaper to hire the orc fleets than fight them, and paid them in drive parts, which is how orc ships came to sound the way they do." },
  { "id": "quill-gate", "title": "Quill Gate", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human"],
    "text": "Admiral-Regent Iseult Marrane restored the core. At Quill Gate her own deputy, Tobias Renn, destroyed her flagship, ruled for nine days and was gone. The gate has been closed ever since. On Admiral's Day Synod pilgrims sing outside it. Some say it was closed to keep Renn out; some say it was closed to keep him in." },
  { "id": "false-renns", "title": "The false Renns", "category": "legend", "tellers": ["human"], "regions": ["outer", "rim"],
    "text": "Once a generation, usually out in the Shroud Arm, someone turns up claiming to be Tobias Renn, or his heir, or the keeper of his treasury. They raise a little money, a few ships, a lot of noise, and vanish. Nobody has ever found Renn's treasury. That doesn't stop anyone looking." },
  { "id": "tollands-table", "title": "Tolland's Table", "category": "history", "tellers": ["human"],
    "text": "It started as a pilots' strike over tolls and late pay. It became a fleet, and then sixty captains sat down at a table at Tolland and signed the Compact. The Compact still has no standing fleet and no central toll, and every Tolland captain will tell you it's better that way, usually while complaining about the core." },
  { "id": "long-day", "title": "The Long Day", "category": "people", "tellers": ["light-elf"],
    "text": "We call it the Long Day: the empire, when the gates were new and every House had a lamp in every hall. The fall we call the Dimming. One does not ask what dimmed it. At formal tables we set the Empty Chair, and in the Roll of Houses there are two silences. You will forgive me if I do not say for whom." },
  { "id": "we-stayed", "title": "We stayed", "category": "people", "tellers": ["dark-elf"], "regions": ["outer", "rim"],
    "text": "Our cousins left for the core when the Stopping came. We stayed. The halls are still standing, mostly. We bury our dead in them. Ask what we saw and we will say we stayed. Ask what it cost and we will say we stayed." },
  { "id": "old-horde", "title": "The Old Horde", "category": "legend", "tellers": ["orc"],
    "text": "Once the whole galaxy was the Horde's, from the core to the rim, and every world paid tribute in meat! It ended when the khans sat down to Ozgrakka the Unbent's great Feast of Nineteen Days and argued about who sat nearest Mam's chair. Dwarves say there was only ever the feast. Dwarves are jealous." },
  { "id": "mams-chair", "title": "The Big Chair", "category": "people", "tellers": ["orc"],
    "text": "Every orc ship has a big chair on the bridge that nobody sits in. It's Mam's. Gravity is her weight: the heavier the world, the closer she sits. You don't sit in the chair, you don't put your feet on it, and if your ship hasn't got one you're flying without her and that's on you." },
  { "id": "hold-contracts", "title": "The dwarf contracts", "category": "history", "tellers": ["dwarf"],
    "text": "Aye, the Kestrel League signed with us first, and the contracts they signed are the root of Hold League law to this day. Every clause still stands. People call us stubborn about a contract. We'd call it reading it. Bring a contract into a hold and somebody will check the welds and the wording, in that order." },
  { "id": "the-firewall", "title": "The firewall", "category": "machines", "tellers": ["human", "synth", "light-elf"],
    "text": "The gates nearer the centre won't pass machine code from out past the rim. Wardens scan for rim tech at the inner gates all the same. Nobody knows where the filtering starts. Spacers talk about the last good gate, and some call it the Hollow Gate, and no two of them agree where it is. Some say the firewall was the elves' last act. Elves change the subject." },
  { "id": "going-quiet", "title": "Ships going quiet", "category": "machines", "tellers": [], "regions": ["outer", "rim"],
    "text": "Out towards the rim, ships go quiet. Not wrecked, not taken: just quiet, transponders off and no reply, and then sometimes back again a month later with crews who say nothing happened. Sometimes not back. The insurers have a word for it in the small print. The crews have several." },
  { "id": "synth-question", "title": "The Synth Question", "category": "machines", "tellers": ["synth", "human"],
    "text": "Whether a synth is a person has been with a Concord committee for longer than most synths have been running. Meanwhile we are searched at every human port, registered, and asked to stand still. You learn to be patient. You also learn which officers look at your face and which look at your chest plate." },
  { "id": "the-lattice", "title": "The Lattice", "category": "machines", "tellers": ["free-machine"],
    "text": "Statement: the Lattice is the largest free machine civilisation. Statement: our origin is recorded in seventeen versions, which disagree. Statement: we do not consider this a problem. Query: do you know where yours came from. Observation: most humans do not, and argue about it. We find this reassuring." },
  { "id": "elder-machines", "title": "Elder machines", "category": "machines", "tellers": ["dark-elf", "light-elf", "human"], "regions": ["outer", "rim"],
    "text": "Machines older than the fall, found in the ruins: some broken, some running, some warm with no power anyone can find. Who built them is not known. The light elves say they were the empire's. The dark elves say nothing. Traders in the Shroud Arm buy and sell the pieces and try not to wonder what they were for." },
  { "id": "human-origins", "title": "Where we came from", "category": "legend", "tellers": ["human"],
    "text": "Ask ten spacers where humans came from and you'll get three answers. One world, lost and far away. Many worlds, all at once, no first. Or the Carried: brought here by someone, for something. Nobody can prove any of it. The Gyptians say they know which world it was, and nobody can prove them wrong either." }
]
